The Security Council,

Recalling its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 446 (1979), 452 (1979), 465 (1980), 476 (1980), 478 (1980), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),

Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force,

Reaffirming the obligation of Israel, the occupying Power, to abide scrupulously by its legal obligations and responsibilities under the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Reaffirming the right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, and stressing that all counter-terrorism measures must comply with international law, including international humanitarian law and human rights law,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, and the tragic loss of civilian lives, including women and children,

Condemning all violence and hostilities against civilians and all acts of terrorism, as well as all indiscriminate attacks against civilians, and stressing the need to protect civilians and ensure accountability,

Gravely concerned by the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including the displacement of civilians, the destruction of civilian infrastructure, the denial of access to food, water, electricity, fuel and medical care, and the inoperability of hospitals,

Stressing the need for safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip, including through the Rafah crossing, and for the protection of humanitarian personnel, medical personnel, United Nations facilities and other civilian objects,

Rejecting any forced displacement of civilians, and stressing the obligation to ensure the safe and voluntary return of those displaced,

Reaffirming its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, on the basis of the 1967 lines and relevant United Nations resolutions, and stressing the need to resume credible negotiations towards a two-State solution,

Stressing that the current crisis must not lead to a regional escalation, and urging all parties and regional actors to exercise restraint,

Noting the diplomatic efforts of the Secretary-General, Member States, regional organizations and humanitarian agencies to de-escalate the situation and provide humanitarian assistance,

1. Calls for an immediate, durable and fully respected humanitarian ceasefire, leading to the cessation of hostilities throughout the Gaza Strip;

2. Reaffirms the right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law;

3. Calls for the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages, and demands that they be treated humanely and in accordance with international humanitarian law;

4. Demands that all parties allow, facilitate and ensure safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need in the Gaza Strip, including by opening humanitarian corridors and crossings, and ensure the continuous delivery of food, water, fuel, medical supplies, electricity and other essential goods and services;

5. Calls for the immediate restoration of essential services, including water, electricity, health care and sanitation, and for the protection of hospitals, medical personnel and United Nations and humanitarian facilities from attack;

6. Condemns all violence, hostilities and indiscriminate attacks against civilians, including the terrorist attacks in Israel on 7 October 2023 and all attacks against civilians in the Gaza Strip, and calls for full respect for international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, precaution and proportionality, and for accountability for all violations;

7. Rejects any forced displacement of Palestinian civilians and any attempts to alter the demographic composition, character and status of the Palestinian territory occupied since 1967, including East Jerusalem, and calls for the safe and voluntary return of displaced persons;

8. Calls upon all parties to exercise maximum restraint, refrain from acts of provocation, incitement and inflammatory rhetoric, and prevent the spillover of the conflict into the region;

9. Reaffirms the importance of the two-State solution based on the 1967 lines and relevant United Nations resolutions, and calls for the resumption of credible negotiations on all final status issues, in accordance with the Madrid terms of reference, the Arab Peace Initiative and the Quartet Roadmap;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 72 hours on the implementation of this resolution, and decides to remain seized of the matter.

